task: sendbulk-profile
agent: opus5-bench
started: 2026-09-09
branch: feature/sendbulk-profile
worktree: /Users/sergiy/work/my/okay-wt-sendbulk

why: the last open investigation from the benchmark refresh. §15 said
  `Channel.sendManyNow` is 1.63x ahead of a per-element send against a
  draining consumer; it measured 17% BEHIND, stably. The obvious
  explanation was tested and REFUTED with numbers — the bulk path is
  exercised almost perfectly (97.3% of calls find room, mean claim
  55.8 of 64 asked, 0.05% of elements fall back) — so the cost is in
  the bulk mechanism itself, and the entry has said "next step is a
  profiler" ever since.

what: profile `okaySendBulkRecvChunk` against `okaySendElemRecvChunk`
  and attribute, then fix what the profile names. Also files, as board
  hygiene, the `flushAfter` observation this evening's re-measure
  turned up: it costs 30% over its own chunked merge where the page
  said 9%, and nothing has looked at it.

predict: none. The standing hypothesis in the backlog — that
  `Ring.pushMany` touches every slot twice, once to scan a stamp and
  once to write, to save a tail CAS that is uncontended with one
  producer — is UNMEASURED, and four of my last six predictions on
  this codebase were wrong. The profile decides.
  DISQUALIFYING: if the profile puts the time in WAITING rather than
  in the scan, the mechanism is not the cost and §15's claim should be
  deleted rather than defended — the element path simply caught up,
  which the doc already half-says.
